{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/253722-the-innate-nature-of-women-heroes","id":253722,"title":"The Innate Nature of Women Heroes","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","Scion"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-09-09T18:08:12-07:00","id":253722,"title":"The Innate Nature of Women Heroes","content":"<span style=\"font-family:Arial\">With the new forums rules in place, I'll preface this topic with the request that if you don\u2019t run your Scion games with the mythology heavily-influencing the actual story, then please do not post examples. All of us are welcomed to (and some do!) play Scion without the mentality of the old ways influencing the game, but this topic is for the intersecting of Old. vs. New</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">That being said!</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">This came to mind in the middle of Women\u2019s History as we\u2019re talking about Ancient Greece and how, for all the marvels and ideals our current society lauds them for \u2026 they were really, REALLY shitty towards women. I\u2019m talking, you aren\u2019t even considered a person type of shitty.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Taking the gods of such a culture and injecting them into modern times, how has your groups coped with such values being commonplace in most Bronze-Age cultures (surprisingly, the Egyptians were rather decent towards women).</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">In my games, I try to keep the misogyny to subtle reminders. The Gods refer to the Bands as under the leadership of the men, the male scions are queried (and blamed) first, and deference goes to them. I\u2019ve had several of my own female characters and female PCs trying to change the status quo, but it\u2019s all punishing uphill. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">How do you handle ancient values and the inherent othering of cultures towards women and those who weren\u2019t exactly the \u2018ideal\u2019\u2019?</span>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/606-aynie","name":"Aynie","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar606_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-09-09T19:11:43-07:00","id":253759,"title":"","content":"I just have a few questions...<br /> <br /> 1. Is it only Gods that refer to the Bands under male leadership, or does that extend to Goddesses as well?<br /> <br /> Actually, I only have that one question.<br /> <br /> I've always looked at it as the Gods view things in terms of power, not gender, and since there's no noticeable power discrepancy between male and female Scions, there's no difference in how they treat the Scions.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/370-oriares","name":"Oriares","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar370_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"404"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-09-09T19:13:53-07:00","id":253761,"title":"","content":"Something to keep in mind about Greek Myth. Hesiod, who was responsible for preserving most of their myths, was a <b>giant</b> misogynist. To the point that <i>other Greeks</i> were even like &quot;Yo, I think he has some issues.&quot; The thing with history, especially that far back, is there's only so many sources available. So if your biggest source on Greek myth is a dude freaking out about how the universe used to be great until those darn GIRLS came around and screwed everything up, it's hard to determine if that was actually the prevailing mythological view or if this guy was just writing a proto-Rodger Manifesto. That being said, you're right in that Greeks in general were pretty awful about women (except the Spartans, who were militaristic to the point of not caring). <br /> <br /> Basically my view on that kind of stuff in games is that historical accuracy always comes second to what players want. If your players want a game where they team up with a reborn Sappho and smash the patriarchy and blare Bratmobile then heck yeah, full speed ahead. If they're sick of dealing with that stuff in their real lives and just want to smash monsters then leave that junk in the past where it belongs. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3738-illuminated-minutia","name":"Illuminated Minutia","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3738_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"280"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-09-09T22:53:32-07:00","id":253854,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Oriares</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/253722-the-innate-nature-of-women-heroes?p=253759#post253759\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I just have a few questions...<br /> <br /> 1. Is it only Gods that refer to the Bands under male leadership, or does that extend to Goddesses as well?<br /> <br /> Actually, I only have that one question.<br /> <br /> I've always looked at it as the Gods view things in terms of power, not gender, and since there's no noticeable power discrepancy between male and female Scions, there's no difference in how they treat the Scions.</div> </div> </div> </div>It depends on the Pantheon they're dealing with. The Greeks do not address the women of the band if it is at all possible. Or rather, the men don't address the women. The goddesses themselves are a little less strigent ... and defer to the male counterparts when they're in the same room. When, say, Hera's alone, it then goes to whoever has captured her attention in the conversation.<br /> <br /> When they met with the Irish and the Egyptians, as those cultures were a little more ... understanding of women as people in their own right, the Gods gave deference to the ladies more. Set, for example, listened to the guardian female scion over the fire-based pretty boy because the woman would be more worthy to listen to in a war-time situation.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> . So if your biggest source on Greek myth is a dude freaking out about how the universe used to be great until those darn GIRLS came around and screwed everything up, it's hard to determine if that was actually the prevailing mythological view or if this guy was just writing a proto-Rodger Manifesto. </div> </div> </div>I base the Theoi more on the Athenian ideal with Ares taking on the Spartan P.O.V. Because of what you said, I try to compare what the prevailing sociological ideas at the time to blend into the Gods themselves.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Basically my view on that kind of stuff in games is that historical accuracy always comes second to what players want. If your players want a game where they team up with a reborn Sappho and smash the patriarchy and blare Bratmobile then heck yeah, full speed ahead. If they're sick of dealing with that stuff in their real lives and just want to smash monsters then leave that junk in the past where it belongs </div> </div> </div>I agree wholeheartedly! I have run both types of games and it depends entirely on the group makeup if I push for the horrible collision of ancient vs. modern. My main player group is all involved heavily in some form of historical or mythological focus and so that group expects and wants the craziness of horrible history.<br /> <br /> My off night player group wants Scion mythology but without the ...less savory aspects of it, and they don't get the craziness.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/606-aynie","name":"Aynie","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar606_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-09-09T23:07:29-07:00","id":253859,"title":"","content":"I would say the way you run it is good. I personally prefer the games mythologically and culturally accurate, even if that means conflicting social mores.<br /> <br /> The way I look at it is that Gods are not vehicles of change by nature (unless of course, they are) and really personify the culture and customs of their specific people, and that means that not only are they anachronistic in many regards, they actually truly believe that they are acting acceptably when they engage in casual Slavery or Homophobia or Sexism in the same way as a Western American might object to it.<br /> <br /> Unless the party takes pains to change this view among the gods, thats my default. Changing such a prevailing cultural view amongst the Gods is also a truly mythic task in its own right.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3835-\u00falfh\u00e9\u00f0nar","name":"\u00dalfh\u00e9\u00f0nar","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"108"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-09-10T01:36:45-07:00","id":253919,"title":"","content":"We've actually had this discussion in game... Zeus, the god of gods, is among the most misogynistic gods of our games.<br /> <br /> We run it so it mostly applies to mortals though... Most gods realize that their plight against the titans are to dire to insult a Scion depending on their gender. Some of the more rowdy gods however, Zeus foremost, tend to go all out and not censor themselves at all.<br /> <br /> There are other things however, that the players has experienced. Thralls, or tr\u00e4lar, who were quite common during the ancient viking era, is still a common thing in the Norse overworld. One player was even kidnapped to be sold as a thrall and a few of the characters agreed with the norse sentiment was valid while others found it revolting. My players all agree: Slavery is bad, but their characters however, had very different opinion about that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/593-gemstone","name":"Gemstone","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"139"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-09-10T04:29:04-07:00","id":253990,"title":"","content":"In my games, which admittedly are heavily \u00c6sir based, this has been less of an issue due to some of the strong females in Norse legend. Freya was not only able to claim a significant share of the dead (which isn't bad, considering that she was more or less a hostage) and Skadi's ability to demand compensation for the death of her father and receive it, rather than the gods basically pushing her over and having Thor hit her was pretty impressive as well.<br /> <br /> In the game the theme has been 'put up or shut up' and any \u00c6sir Scion is expected to go out and prove their stuff if they want to be invited to the big kids table. Gender hasn't been an issue, which has gladly been a bit borne out by the recent (if currently up for debate) discoveries of real world Norse remains that suggest a significant number of the warriors were women.<br /> <br /> We don't really wander far outside the \u00c6sir so I'm afraid I can't say much about RP of women from any other pantheon.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2608-squee","name":"Squee","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2608_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"120"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-09-10T04:31:01-07:00","id":253991,"title":"","content":"It's a tough one. The way I would deal (or have dealt) with bias against the genders is by emphasising that although this culture might have a bias against women/men, there is also a standard to be met for the other gender. For example is Zeus going to be more annoyed by a woman speaking to him versus a man being &quot;un-greek&quot;? He might find the latter much more intolerable, to the point of ignoring the former.<br /> <br /> Also gender roles can be quite different from today's (Irish gods taken as I know them best). As an example, in Irish law a &quot;husband&quot; was a marital role that was usually filled by the male and was granted more authority and status. However there were well defined legal contexts in which the female in the relationship could be the husband, particularly if the male had no land and she did, or the male came from a low ranking tuatha (clan). The needle of a skilled craftswoman was valuable beyond a doctor's tools or a bards library in certain contexts.<br /> <br /> I guess I'm saying that since the gender roles were so different anyway, in my experience they tend to naturally downplay sexism. I mean if a Irish god says &quot;Stop talking about love and romance, do you think you're a man?!&quot; to a female character it's sexist, but different. Of course this isn't always the answer, but it can be helpful.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3326-an-fhuiseog","name":"An Fhuiseog","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3326_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"271"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-09-10T05:22:51-07:00","id":254019,"title":"","content":"Scion seems to work on the assumption that gods have taken on roles in the world. The texts of the original core book mentions Anubis as a body guard for example. It's not entierly unreasonable that the gods have changed over time and that their behaviour and values have changed. On the other hand, gods are gods... if fate decreed that god YYY is the most sexist god of all, that might be very difficult to change...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/593-gemstone","name":"Gemstone","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"139"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-09-10T10:24:06-07:00","id":254176,"title":"","content":"In our games we felt playing historically accurate for a woman would be less fun for those doing so. We went with the approach of how are the gods represented (i.e. if you want rain you pray to Zeus, if you want to fall in love you pray to Aphrodite) to work out some of the balances of power. <br /> <br /> I also feel that it\u2019s a lot of negative sides of the Greek/Roman pantheon that get remembered but very little of how women were represented in a positive light that ever get taken seriously or mentioned. For instance, there were at one time six members of the Olympian council that were women a full half until Dionysus came on board and Hestia gave up her seat. That is some pretty impressive female representation. <br /> <br /> Now maybe its because I\u2019m Greek but I felt I had to illustrate that point for an ancient Greek/Roman campaign we ran. Women could be important, powerful, badass warriors, brilliant strategists, competent in any field they set their mind to and just as capable as any man. It wasn\u2019t an idyllic setting, there were still negatives obviously, some men would treat women poorly, people cheated on their spouces, etc. but here are some of the goddesses that showed up or were talked about/prayed to:<br /> <br /> Mnemosyne: she created writing and languages.<br /> <br /> The Mousai: her daughters that represented all the arts.<br /> <br /> Athena: extremely skilled and competent, educated, wise, beautiful, brilliant and beat Ares every time they clashed; beat Poseidon out for Athens.<br /> <br /> Nike: Zeus' badass bodyguard and spirit of victory.<br /> <br /> Nemesis: the goddess of vengeance and retribution.<br /> <br /> The Morai: the Fates who even the gods pay deference to.<br /> <br /> Nyx: ridiculously powerful and terrifying who even an angry Zeus backed up from.<br /> <br /> Dike &amp; Themis: personifying Justice<br /> <br /> Styx upon whom all sacred and terrible oaths were sworn. <br /> <br /> Arete the spirit of excellence and effectiveness that all the Greeks strive for and also the goddess of valour.<br /> <br /> The Charites: that represented social grace and mirth bringing joy to everyone.<br /> <br /> Of course that is a short list and there were as many negative representations of female gods as there were positive but in this short list it is women that represent each of the Greek virtues: Mnemosyne &amp; Athena (Intellect), The Mousai (Expression), Nemesis (Vengeance) and Arete who is both excellence and valour. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/333-achilles","name":"Achilles","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar333_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"81"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-09-10T10:44:02-07:00","id":254188,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>\u00dalfh\u00e9\u00f0nar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/253722-the-innate-nature-of-women-heroes?p=253859#post253859\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I would say the way you run it is good. I personally prefer the games mythologically and culturally accurate, even if that means conflicting social mores.<br /> <br /> The way I look at it is that Gods are not vehicles of change by nature (unless of course, they are) and really personify the culture and customs of their specific people, and that means that not only are they anachronistic in many regards, they actually truly believe that they are acting acceptably when they engage in casual Slavery or Homophobia or Sexism in the same way as a Western American might object to it.<br /> <br /> Unless the party takes pains to change this view among the gods, thats my default. Changing such a prevailing cultural view amongst the Gods is also a truly mythic task in its own right.</div> </div> </div> </div>See, for me I don't like the idea of gods always holding the 'classic' era of their views - some would, of course, but that's more a reflection of their personality than their 'nature' - particularly since it makes no sense in relation to gods who cultures and customs still very much exist, such as Orisha/Loa or various Asian deities. How can the gods not react and adapt to the culture they supposedly fully encompass, especially when they are supposed to walk amongst mortals, like, all the time?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/209-that-other-guy","name":"That Other Guy","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"702"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-09-11T11:04:22-07:00","id":254423,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aynie</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/253722-the-innate-nature-of-women-heroes#post253722\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">How do you handle ancient values and the inherent othering of cultures towards women and those who weren\u2019t exactly the \u2018ideal\u2019\u2019?</div> </div> </div> </div>This depends on how I view the relationship between the pantheon and the worshipers. If the &quot;power&quot; is one way, pantheon to whorshiper then the ideals and views have not changed much in the past millenea. And I would not run this unless the group could/would find some enjoyment in this mattter.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, if the whorshipers have influence on the pantheon, then the pantheon would change over time and be more modern in their thinking. Maybe a century behind which would allow for some interesting conflict but still not the DESTROY A CITY BECAUSE SOMEONE COMPARED THEMSELVES TO A GOD type of actiions.<br /> <br /> It does not seem likely that a pantheon could keep the same level of whorship without giving some ground when their basic followers attitudes change.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>while (horse.state == dead)<br /> {<br /> beat();<br /> }","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/830-amdramnar","name":"Amdramnar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar830_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"135"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-09-11T14:28:00-07:00","id":254616,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>That Other Guy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/253722-the-innate-nature-of-women-heroes?p=254188#post254188\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> See, for me I don't like the idea of gods always holding the 'classic' era of their views - some would, of course, but that's more a reflection of their personality than their 'nature' - particularly since it makes no sense in relation to gods who cultures and customs still very much exist, such as Orisha/Loa or various Asian deities. How can the gods not react and adapt to the culture they supposedly fully encompass, especially when they are supposed to walk amongst mortals, like, all the time?</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> The idea that Gods walk among mortals all the time is entirely schizophrenic in Scion, where its canon that they are immensely busy with the threat of titans and internal and external politics to the point where A Hero should feel honored to get some of their time, but that they still have day jobs. One of these has to give.<br /> <br /> A God is not likely to be swayed by the opinions of men, in fact, mostly of them could convince ANY mortal that slavery is morally acceptable on the spot if they chose to. That said, many of the cultural deities do in fact have modern day counterparts and active worshippers, but your assuming that just because they live in the modern day that they share modern western morals and beliefs. The west africans who worship the Orisha are largely, from the western perspective, incredibly sexist and still possess tribal loyalties. The Japanese are likewise Xenophobic and are just now coming off blatant sexism. There are Chinese nationals who still live in the traditional way of the Middle Kingdom. This does not even take into account all the little views and perspectives that they differ with.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3835-\u00falfh\u00e9\u00f0nar","name":"\u00dalfh\u00e9\u00f0nar","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"108"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-09-11T14:30:48-07:00","id":254619,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Squee</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/253722-the-innate-nature-of-women-heroes?p=253990#post253990\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In the game the theme has been 'put up or shut up' and any \u00c6sir Scion is expected to go out and prove their stuff if they want to be invited to the big kids table. Gender hasn't been an issue, which has gladly been a bit borne out by the recent (if currently up for debate) discoveries of real world Norse remains that suggest a significant number of the warriors were women.</div> </div> </div> </div>Thats an internet rumour that skews the findings of the study. It found that 6/13 SETTLERS were women, it had nothing to do with the composition of warriors, which the Sagas, Myths and Laws of the time indicate that while women generally received some martial training to defend the home, they were not warriors in the same vein as men and certainly not in formal war where the use of shield walls would be severely compromised by their relative size and strength.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3835-\u00falfh\u00e9\u00f0nar","name":"\u00dalfh\u00e9\u00f0nar","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"108"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-09-11T16:34:55-07:00","id":254771,"title":"","content":"So I see. That's a bit disappointing. Still, 'put up or shut up' remains a theme for the Norse ladies in the game.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2608-squee","name":"Squee","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2608_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"120"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-09-11T20:00:46-07:00","id":254972,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>That Other Guy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/253722-the-innate-nature-of-women-heroes?p=254188#post254188\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> See, for me I don't like the idea of gods always holding the 'classic' era of their views - some would, of course, but that's more a reflection of their personality than their 'nature' - particularly since it makes no sense in relation to gods who cultures and customs still very much exist, such as Orisha/Loa or various Asian deities. How can the gods not react and adapt to the culture they supposedly fully encompass, especially when they are supposed to walk amongst mortals, like, all the time?</div> </div> </div> </div>We're completely opposite then.<br /> <br /> I run Scion (and have a group of players who play Scion) to interact with the Gods of the Mythologies we adore as they were(are). Hera is always going to start out with a raging murder-hate for any bastard scions of her wayward husband. The Norse as a whole will probably be way too concerned about Global Warming. The Egyptians are in a very cold civil war, and the Morrigu is quite frankly insane and powerful.<br /> <br /> Think about another thing for the Gods: Time and the passage of it. Think about to summer afternoons when you were a kid and how they almost never ended. They dragged on in glorious adventure and fun. When you were in 1st grade, that summer was 1/6th of your life. Once that kid hits college, that same length of time is now roughly 1/18th to 1/20th of their lifetime. It goes by quicker.<br /> <br /> Now think of a God living for 2000+ years, shoot, the Sumerians go even further to around 6000+ years. A summer is 1/60000 of their time. It's a blink. 60 years is just a blink a fraction of a second longer. They're not going to give any remote inkling of a care to human events or the way the world turns because as soon as they notice it (if they ever do) it's going to go away in the blink right after that first one.<br /> <br /> It's only once the PCs are within the game and interacting with the Gods on a regular basis that personalities shift and evolve. Human values and human morals aren't strong enough to shape the belief of immortal, near-all-powerful entities who were never really human in the first place; but the dramatic effect of a Scion can and will shape the world around them as they're shaped by it.<br /> <br /> Being a lady myself who is routinely driven to fits of rage over women's history, I can assure you that misogyny doesn't last forever in games i tend to run or play in, but it does not go away over night, or sometimes, ever go away entirely. PCs have to fight to change the divine societies they're a part of, and perhaps even bring them into the modern setting and continue to push them beyond that. It's something that has become a huge underlying theme in any of my games, be it social issues or just teaching Zeus that constantly cheating on Hera is a LITTLE uncool.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/606-aynie","name":"Aynie","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar606_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-09-11T22:55:51-07:00","id":255069,"title":"","content":"I run my own games under the assumption that the myths as existed were more diverse than the ones that patriarchal authority actively preserved. Women's oral traditions and how they differ from much of cultural &quot;canon&quot; is an active area of anthropological study - though obvious much of the actual content is forever lost to us.<br /> <br /> When in doubt, lean toward diversity in the Pantheon. Zeus has a definite history of rape, and Hera a vengeful victim-blamer - but I wouldn't have Athena curse anyone for being raped (the gorgons are just straight up Titan-spawn, via the monster Ceto as the Greeks described; that whole Roman story of the cursed priestess of Minerva story was made up whole-cloth by Ovid - at most Athena cursed a beautiful monster to reveal her true face); and Artemis is down right vindictive in the defense of women from men.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-09-12T12:20:13-07:00","id":255482,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>\u00dalfh\u00e9\u00f0nar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/253722-the-innate-nature-of-women-heroes?p=254616#post254616\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> The idea that Gods walk among mortals all the time is entirely schizophrenic in Scion, where its canon that they are immensely busy with the threat of titans and internal and external politics to the point where A Hero should feel honored to get some of their time, but that they still have day jobs. One of these has to give.<br /> <br /> A God is not likely to be swayed by the opinions of men, in fact, mostly of them could convince ANY mortal that slavery is morally acceptable on the spot if they chose to. That said, many of the cultural deities do in fact have modern day counterparts and active worshippers, but your assuming that just because they live in the modern day that they share modern western morals and beliefs. The west africans who worship the Orisha are largely, from the western perspective, incredibly sexist and still possess tribal loyalties. The Japanese are likewise Xenophobic and are just now coming off blatant sexism. There are Chinese nationals who still live in the traditional way of the Middle Kingdom. This does not even take into account all the little views and perspectives that they differ with.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ok, first, that line about the Yoruba who worship the Orisha is complete nonsense, coming from someone who knows more than a few Yoruba who follow the traditions. Tribal loyalties are still a big part of West African culture not merely because of mythological status, but as a symbol of a shared culture - the idea that 'tribal loyalties' being an inherently bad thing is an incredibly Eurocentric idea that is there to homogenise Africans into a single lump, not many distinct cultures that were forced together into 'countries' due to Western imperialism. Being proud of one's tribe or people is no different from being proud of your country (and I say this as an Esan).<br /> <br /> Secondly, you're the one that brought up the idea that the God is a representation of their culture, therefore if the culture changes (such as they have) therefore so to must the gods.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/209-that-other-guy","name":"That Other Guy","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"702"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-09-12T13:05:00-07:00","id":255525,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>\u00dalfh\u00e9\u00f0nar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/253722-the-innate-nature-of-women-heroes?p=254616#post254616\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The idea that Gods walk among mortals all the time is entirely schizophrenic in Scion, where its canon that they are immensely busy with the threat of titans and internal and external politics to the point where A Hero should feel honored to get some of their time, but that they still have day jobs. One of these has to give.<br /> <br /> A God is not likely to be swayed by the opinions of men, in fact, mostly of them could convince ANY mortal that slavery is morally acceptable on the spot if they chose to. That said, many of the cultural deities do in fact have modern day counterparts and active worshippers, but your assuming that just because they live in the modern day that they share modern western morals and beliefs. The west africans who worship the Orisha are largely, from the western perspective, incredibly sexist and still possess tribal loyalties. The Japanese are likewise Xenophobic and are just now coming off blatant sexism. There are Chinese nationals who still live in the traditional way of the Middle Kingdom. This does not even take into account all the little views and perspectives that they differ with.</div> </div> </div> </div><span style=\"color:red\">lets not call other cultures sexist or xenophobic, please.</span>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-09-12T20:51:35-07:00","id":255780,"title":"","content":"I tend to play towards the stereotypes, but with a little more research and that they are still alive in the modern age. Like Zeus's romps were not always what modern civilization defines as rape. In ancient Greek, it was rape if you didn't get permission of the man that had legal authority over the woman (ie. father's and husbands). That doesn't mean that Zeus wasn't a bit of a man slut and has a hard time hearing 'no' but that not all of his 'victims' were actually unwilling partners.<br /> <br /> And that they have grown up with the times a bit. They aren't going to forget their roots, but they won't normally go ancient pillaging and looting, either.<br /> <br /> Ares/Mars is a bit of an ass and doesn't normally visit his female scions, Athena and Artemis will only adopt girls, etc. (One of my players was a female scion of Ares, but it was a trick of Athena and Aphrodite while Ares was a bit busy with something else.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1246-arthurhansen","name":"ArthurHansen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1246_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"66"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-09-13T07:51:55-07:00","id":256040,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArthurHansen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/253722-the-innate-nature-of-women-heroes?p=255780#post255780\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I tend to play towards the stereotypes, but with a little more research and that they are still alive in the modern age. Like Zeus's romps were not always what modern civilization defines as rape. In ancient Greek, it was rape if you didn't get permission of the man that had legal authority over the woman (ie. father's and husbands). That doesn't mean that Zeus wasn't a bit of a man slut and has a hard time hearing 'no' but that not all of his 'victims' were actually unwilling partners.<br /> <br /> And that they have grown up with the times a bit. They aren't going to forget their roots, but they won't normally go ancient pillaging and looting, either.<br /> <br /> Ares/Mars is a bit of an ass and doesn't normally visit his female scions, Athena and Artemis will only adopt girls, etc. (One of my players was a female scion of Ares, but it was a trick of Athena and Aphrodite while Ares was a bit busy with something else.)</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Could you enlighten me as to why you have Athena only adopt female Scions? Looking at the myths, and imagining the Greek heroes as Scions, Odysseus is very much Athena's to my mind.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/370-oriares","name":"Oriares","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar370_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"404"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-09-13T22:52:37-07:00","id":256535,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Oriares</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/253722-the-innate-nature-of-women-heroes?p=256040#post256040\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> Could you enlighten me as to why you have Athena only adopt female Scions? Looking at the myths, and imagining the Greek heroes as Scions, Odysseus is very much Athena's to my mind.</div> </div> </div> </div>The actual scions (gods) that are listed as hers, actually. I think it also mentioned it, but it might have been inference between Artemis, Demeter and Athena only adopting. I think it was explicit that Artemis only adopted females, but I felt it was insinuated that they did too.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1246-arthurhansen","name":"ArthurHansen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1246_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"66"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-09-14T00:32:14-07:00","id":256555,"title":"","content":"I would probably add Hera to that list too. As a goddess of marriage and fidelity I couldn't see her cheating, even to assist the war effort. Wouldn't stop her asking other gods to sire kids on her behalf, to adopt, though.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2608-squee","name":"Squee","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2608_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"120"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-09-14T08:20:46-07:00","id":256646,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArthurHansen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/253722-the-innate-nature-of-women-heroes?p=256535#post256535\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The actual scions (gods) that are listed as hers, actually. I think it also mentioned it, but it might have been inference between Artemis, Demeter and Athena only adopting. I think it was explicit that Artemis only adopted females, but I felt it was insinuated that they did too.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> It would be grossly out of character for Athena to only adopt women - that was definitely talking about Artemis.<br /> <br /> I'm assuming you mean Hestia, not Demeter. Demeter isn't a virgin goddess (she's mother of Persephone).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-09-14T13:05:07-07:00","id":256769,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>glamourweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/253722-the-innate-nature-of-women-heroes?p=256646#post256646\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> It would be grossly out of character for Athena to only adopt women - that was definitely talking about Artemis.<br /> <br /> I'm assuming you mean Hestia, not Demeter. Demeter isn't a virgin goddess (she's mother of Persephone).</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I don't even see that in the book for Artemis, even though it does make perfect sense for her.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/370-oriares","name":"Oriares","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar370_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"404"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-04T01:58:40-07:00","id":272117,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aynie</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/253722-the-innate-nature-of-women-heroes#post253722\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">With the new forums rules in place, I'll preface this topic with the request that if you don\u2019t run your Scion games with the mythology heavily-influencing the actual story, then please do not post examples. All of us are welcomed to (and some do!) play Scion without the mentality of the old ways influencing the game, but this topic is for the intersecting of Old. vs. New</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">That being said!</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">This came to mind in the middle of Women\u2019s History as we\u2019re talking about Ancient Greece and how, for all the marvels and ideals our current society lauds them for \u2026 they were really, REALLY shitty towards women. I\u2019m talking, you aren\u2019t even considered a person type of shitty.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Taking the gods of such a culture and injecting them into modern times, how has your groups coped with such values being commonplace in most Bronze-Age cultures (surprisingly, the Egyptians were rather decent towards women).</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">In my games, I try to keep the misogyny to subtle reminders. The Gods refer to the Bands as under the leadership of the men, the male scions are queried (and blamed) first, and deference goes to them. I\u2019ve had several of my own female characters and female PCs trying to change the status quo, but it\u2019s all punishing uphill. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">How do you handle ancient values and the inherent othering of cultures towards women and those who weren\u2019t exactly the \u2018ideal\u2019\u2019?</span></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Didn't the Greek Goddess of virginity eviscerate any male who tried to seduce her? Now was that Artemis or Athena? I can't remember. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <br /> Besides, if you don't include all the crazy shit that goes down with these pantheons, then it's just another super hero game. Its much more fun when someone in your band is a trickster who's been taking lessons from Daddy Loki.<br /> <br /> <br /> And hey, if their parents refuse to change their ways, the kids can just move out and found their own religion with a more modern day approach.<br /> <br /> The pantheons are from a different era... like grandparents when they say racist shit at dinner. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1394,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","user":"Nyrufa","date":". 10-04-2014, 05:24 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-04T16:32:33-07:00","id":272563,"title":"","content":"<i>\u2018It is the best for all tame animals to be ruled by human beings. For this is how they are kept alive. In the same way, the relationship between the male and the female is <i>by nature</i> such that the male is higher, the female lower, that the male rules and the female is ruled.\u2019<br /> <br /> \u2018That person is by nature a slave who can belong to another person and who only takes part in thinking by recognising it, but not by possessing it. Other living beings (animals) cannot recognise thinking; they just obey feelings. However, there is little difference between using slaves and using tame animals: both provide bodily help to do necessary things.\u2019<br /> <br /> -- Aristotle, <i>Politica</i>, ed. Loeb Classical Library, 1254 b 10-14.<br /> </i><br /> --------<br /> <br /> Now if can find that part where women aren't supposed to have souls....","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-04T17:56:43-07:00","id":272601,"title":"","content":"I think it should be kept in mind that while many of these old pantheons considered women inferior, the goddesses weren't exactly saints either. Aphrodite, the goddess of love, turned poor Medusa into a hideous monster because Poseidon raped her in the goddess' temple. Another turned a woman into a spider just because she beat her in a sewing contest. The Goddesses could be just as cruel and psychotic as their male counterparts. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/redface.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Embarrassment\" smilieid=\"2\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-04T18:22:46-07:00","id":272606,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyrufa</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/253722-the-innate-nature-of-women-heroes?p=272601#post272601\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Aphrodite, the goddess of love, turned poor Medusa into a hideous monster because Poseidon raped her in the goddess' temple. <br /> Another turned a woman into a spider just because she beat her in a sewing contest. The Goddesses could be just as cruel and psychotic as their male counterparts. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/redface.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Embarrassment\" smilieid=\"2\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div>Pretty sure both times that was Athena.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-04T19:13:55-07:00","id":272634,"title":"","content":"Maybe Athena turned the girl into a spider (been years since I read the story), but I could have sworn Aphrodite is the one who made Medusa.<br /> <br /> <br /> Just throwing this out there, but I want to a Scion of the Fates (yeah, enjoy that mental image) who's &quot;gift&quot; from their parent is a glass eye that sees the future. The catch... they have to pluck out one of their eyes to replace it with.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1394,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","user":"Nyrufa","date":". 10-04-2014, 10:23 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-04T19:41:40-07:00","id":272670,"title":"","content":"No, both of those were Athena.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/370-oriares","name":"Oriares","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar370_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"404"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-04T20:01:58-07:00","id":272680,"title":"","content":"Hmmm, I guess she didn't like mortal women getting more attention than her? <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/frown.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Frown\" smilieid=\"11\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-04T20:55:46-07:00","id":272695,"title":"","content":"Well, the gods can be jealous creatures.<br /> I admit when I realized just how negative this particular goddess of civilization can be, I was a bit disappointed.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-04T23:46:15-07:00","id":272779,"title":"","content":"People take the story of Arachne way out of context. It might sound dumb, but the version I've always read had her dead from suicide <i>before </i>Athena turned her into the first Spider. In that light it hardly seems like petty revenge and more like coping with driving a former student to suicide.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><span style=\"color:orangered\">Duly Elected Guild Hierarch</span> of the Exalted Forum<br /> <a href=\"http://northernfoxgames.com/\" target=\"_blank\">http://northernfoxgames.com/</a></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/331-the-revenge-of-tv-head","name":"The Revenge of TV Head","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar331_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1272"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-05T05:20:58-07:00","id":272843,"title":"","content":"The story I read said that Arachne was extraordinarily vain in her ability as a seamstress that she said nobody could match her skill. Athena decided to deal a blow to her pride and challenge her publicly to a contest. Unfortunately it seemed that Athena was just as vain in her own ability, because Arachne won the contest, and Athena was disgraced in front of an entire crowd of mortals. Lashing out in anger, she turned Arachne into a spider.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-05T06:41:58-07:00","id":272905,"title":"","content":"There are a <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arachne#Versions\" target=\"_blank\">few versions</a> floating around. The link doesn't say exactly where the other versions came from, but the one typically read is from Ovid.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-06T05:17:03-07:00","id":273636,"title":"","content":"Celtics and Norse both gave the women at least nearly equal footing, but in different responsibilities. Amatsukami is lead by a goddess. The Deva and Bureaucracy also have a lot of important women.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3961-thrythlind","name":"Thrythlind","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"214"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-06T05:18:57-07:00","id":273637,"title":"","content":"Apparently a viking's mother might have been his first battle instructor in many cases.<br /> <br /> Also, look into the legend of Chu Chulain. His mentor was a master warrior, sorceress and more. I forget the name, which one of my friends would likely kill me for.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3961-thrythlind","name":"Thrythlind","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"214"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-06T05:56:14-07:00","id":273686,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thrythlind</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/253722-the-innate-nature-of-women-heroes?p=273637#post273637\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Also, look into the legend of Chu Chulain. His mentor was a master warrior, sorceress and more. I forget the name, which one of my friends would likely kill me for.</div> </div> </div> </div>Sc\u00e1thach, aka the &quot;Shadow.&quot;","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-06T09:28:03-07:00","id":273925,"title":"","content":"So I'm more familiar with Greek mythos than I am Viking, but weren't the Valkyries treated well? Race of warrior women who escorted worthy souls to Valhalla. I don't think most men would want to piss off the girl who decides if you get into the party after you die.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-06T11:18:04-07:00","id":274054,"title":"","content":"If you're looking for a badass Aesir woman, take a look at Skadi. <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ska%C3%B0i\" target=\"_blank\">http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ska%C3%B0i</a> <br /> She won't be sitting alone in your house waiting for her husband, people. She'll take her bow and her skis, and she'll go roaming the mountain defending her territory and her family. Plus, her relationship with her husband Njord is surprisingly healthy and mature for a divine wedding.<br /> <br /> And yes, all norse women were at least trained to defend herselves. it was mandatory for the parents to teach their kids how to fight. At least for the bondi, the free men. The slaves were another story, of course.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;And for my next trick&quot; I said, &quot;anvils!&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2016-evilmarmelade","name":"Evilmarmelade","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2016_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"632"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-06T16:40:20-07:00","id":274270,"title":"","content":"Yeah, moderately surprising that the Norse were the most progressive European culture in regards to gender equality. <br /> <br /> Women could hold property. <br /> Women were respected as able to protect themselves. <br /> Rape was considered a reprehensible crime. <br /> Sexually Aggressive men and women were not looked upon any differently. <br /> <br /> Raw physical differences were considered but they were a culture that respected a cunning mind as well as a strong body and didn't respect anybody who just say back and did nothing. <br /> <br /> A lot of our history of sexual inequality comes from Rome and Greece. A lot of Western sexual hang ups comes from the fact that one of the most recognized Christian philosophers, St Augustine, had a severely hedonistic youth that came close to killing him and rather than accept that he went too far himself decided that pleasure and sex themselves were evil traps later in life. <br /> <br /> Not to say that the Norse didn't have their problems, of course. <br /> <br /> The best possible culture to live in at the time would probably still resemble some of the worst places today. But with the Norse at least sexual bigotry was relatively minimal.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3961-thrythlind","name":"Thrythlind","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"214"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-06T17:52:24-07:00","id":274323,"title":"","content":"And Norse women could also ask for divorce, and they had to do it in public, humiliating her ex-husband by throwing his weapons out of the house. And while female jarl were really rare, they weren't unheard of.<br /> Really, the only thing that was forbidden to them was the thing, the assembly of free men. They didn't have a say in it unless they were needed for a testimony.<br /> <br /> (The Norse were far more intolerant of homosexuality. The worst way to insult a man was to imply that he's a bottom. The act of sodomy was even punishable by death, at least for the one receiving it.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;And for my next trick&quot; I said, &quot;anvils!&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2016-evilmarmelade","name":"Evilmarmelade","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2016_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"632"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-06T22:15:04-07:00","id":274499,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Evilmarmelade</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/253722-the-innate-nature-of-women-heroes?p=274323#post274323\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> (The Norse were far more intolerant of homosexuality. The worst way to insult a man was to imply that he's a bottom. The act of sodomy was even punishable by death, at least for the one receiving it.)</div> </div> </div> </div>If I'm not mistaken, they used the phrase &quot;Womanly&quot; to refer to gay men. Now I can't remember where I read this, or if it's even accurate. But didn't Norse mythology suggest that Odin was womanly, or at least went both ways? <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/eek.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"EEK!\" smilieid=\"9\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-06T23:35:28-07:00","id":274521,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyrufa</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/253722-the-innate-nature-of-women-heroes?p=274499#post274499\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> If I'm not mistaken, they used the phrase &quot;Womanly&quot; to refer to gay men. Now I can't remember where I read this, or if it's even accurate. But didn't Norse mythology suggest that Odin was womanly, or at least went both ways? <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/eek.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"EEK!\" smilieid=\"9\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> The word is N<i>\u00ed\u00f0 (Pronounced Nith) and basically means &quot;Bad&quot; or not acceptable, also frequently used to mean cowardly or unmanly (literally, not befitting a proper man).<br /> <br /> The idea that Odin is womanly comes from the Lokasenna where Loki gets Drunk and starts insulting all the Gods and Goddesses, it was basically a mortal insult as in Norse Law when someone insinuated that you were a Homosexual you were required to immediately respond with Lethal force to the insult as if they had just tried to kill you. Its often confused with the story that Odin learned Seithr, a form of magic practiced only by women (The Norse were distrustful of Magic and thought it N\u00ed\u00f0, cowardly and not befitting a proper man).<br /> <br /> The Norse (At least the early Norse) used a form of prison rape type situation to so thoroughly Humiliate and Un-Man a defeated enemy that they could never be a threat again due to the stigma surrounding the act. In a way, its similar to how the Greeks/Persians used Blinding and Castration as a punishment. It goes without saying, this was not what we would today refer to as a Homosexual Relationship.</i><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3835-\u00falfh\u00e9\u00f0nar","name":"\u00dalfh\u00e9\u00f0nar","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"108"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-08T22:45:14-08:00","id":334842,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aynie</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/253722-the-innate-nature-of-women-heroes#post253722\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">How do you handle ancient values and the inherent othering of cultures towards women and those who weren\u2019t exactly the \u2018ideal\u2019\u2019?</span></div> </div> </div> </div>In all honesty, I simply don't address it. From my understanding the Gods didn't go away and then suddenly return to find the world changed, they watched it happen (even if they didn't pay a whole lot of attention all the time and, in many cases, only watched their little portion). They've evolved along with the world and some Gods in even the more staid Pantheons have eagerly dived into the World through the years.<br /> <br /> In my games the Gods recognise that things have changed. Some individuals may be more or less sexist than others, but as Pantheons they've recognised the need to change alongside the human cultures. Particularly because those human cultures have more of an impact on the Gods than they'd like, via Mortal Reverence.<br /> <br /> I have no doubt that some Pantheons have accepted modern sexual and racial equality (such as it is) somewhat more freely than others, but I don't address the issue directly unless I specifically want a game focusing on it. As Hillary Flammond (played by Lucy Gutteridge) put it in <i>Top Secret!</i>: 'Things change. People change. Hairstyles change. Interest rates fluctuate.'","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4490-kioma","name":"Kioma","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4490_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}